  "preamble": "The shift from the mid-back rounded vowel [o] to the high-back rounded vowel [u] is a diachronic vowel raising change documented across multiple language families. It typically occurs as part of a chain shift (e.g., the Great Vowel Shift in English) or as a wholesale merger of long mid vowels with high vowels (Proto-Mayan, Historical Polish). Note: unstressed o→u in Portuguese and Catalan is phonologically conditioned and borders on allophony, but the primary documentation here is historical. In Paunaka (Arawakan, Bolivia) *o was raised to u while the older *u moved to central ɨ, so the two back vowels stayed distinct.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Vowel Raising, Great Vowel Shift, Vowel Reduction",
